Maya: Weird one - observed a thundering refresh + request timeouts when a token expires under heavy load on emulator API 34. Calls get CallTimeouts, and we see duplicate refreshes.
Nate: Can you share the interceptor/auth flow?
Maya: Minimal repro in sampleapp: interceptor does runBlocking { tokenRepo.refreshIfNeeded() } and we also set a per-call CallTimeout(2s). If the refresh stalls, OkHttp's CallTimeout cancels the call while refresh is still running which causes multiple concurrent callers to trigger refresh again.
Rina: That matches starvation + single-flight issue. Also if your token refresh blocks an OkHttp thread, the Dispatcher thread pool gets starved and other calls queue up => cascading timeouts.
Maya: Snippet of problematic code:
```
class AuthInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val token = runBlocking { tokenRepo.getOrRefresh() }
    return chain.proceed(chain.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer $token").build())
  }
}
```
Maya: Under load you get many callers hitting runBlocking at the same time. CallTimeout will cancel one call, but refresh continues, and other callers still call refresh too.
Nate: Two action items: (1) Add single-flight refresh primitive in Kotlin sample (suspend function that coalesces concurrent refreshes). (2) Update docs: warn about CallTimeout interaction with blocking network hooks and Dispatcher starvation.
Rina: Also recommend using an in-memory synchronous cache for fast path and kick off background refresh (or use single-flight suspend helper). If you must block in interceptor, ensure refresh uses Dispatchers.IO and limits concurrent refresh attempts.
Maya: Repro steps: run load test against sampleapp endpoints with tokens expiring -> observe many 408/timeout logs and repeated refresh attempts in logs. I attached a small repro script in the PR.
